        Utah Business Tax Penalty Attorneys
      </h2>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6">
        Our tax penalty attorneys can represent you to the IRS and work to abate your penalties. Business tax penalties can represent a massive additional financial burden on top of an already difficult tax situation.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        Common Business Tax Penalties
      </h3>
      <ul class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6 space-y-3 list-disc pl-6">
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Failure to Deposit Penalty</strong> — For late or incorrect payroll tax deposits, ranging from 2% to 15% of the underpayment depending on how late the deposit was made</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Failure to File Penalty</strong> — 5% per month of unpaid tax, up to 25%</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Failure to Pay Penalty</strong> — 0.5% per month of unpaid tax, up to 25%</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Trust Fund Recovery Penalty (TFRP)</strong> — Equal to 100% of undeposited payroll taxes, assessed personally against responsible persons</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Accuracy-Related Penalties</strong> — 20–40% of underpayments due to negligence or substantial understatement</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#C1272D] font-bold">Civil Fraud Penalty</strong> — 75% of underpayment attributable to fraud</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        Penalty Abatement Options
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-4">
        The IRS has several mechanisms to abate (reduce or eliminate) penalties:
      </p>
      <ul class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6 space-y-2 list-disc pl-6">
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">First-Time Penalty Abatement (FTA)</strong> — Available to businesses with a clean compliance history for the three prior years</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Reasonable Cause Abatement</strong> — Available when the failure was due to reasonable cause and not willful neglect (examples: natural disaster, serious illness, reliance on incorrect professional advice)</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Statutory Exceptions</strong> — Certain penalties are automatically waived in qualifying circumstances</li>
        <li><strong class="text-[#1A1A1A]">Correction of IRS Error</strong> — When the penalty was incorrectly assessed</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-[22px] lg:text-[24px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
        Why Professional Help Matters
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65] mb-6">
        Business penalty abatement requests must be carefully documented and argued. The IRS denies many poorly presented requests. Our attorneys know the specific language, documentation, and arguments that are most effective in securing penalty relief.
      </p>
